Jove wrote:
>   Conducts seem to be in Nethack to allow added challenge to expert
> players.  You may not be at that level yet, especially if you're asking
> for help here.
>
>   I.e.: Don't play conducts you're not ready for.

I disagree with this to an extent. I think playing with conducts is to
a certain degree like weight training. Giving yourself a handicap
trains you to overprepare for a situation. When the weights come off,
everything seems easier by contrast. That kind of training can boost
your overall game.

I think this is why so many people ascend their first valk after
playing dozens of games as a rogue, healer, or tourist. When you apply
those survival lessons to a much hardier character, they go far.

The danger, I think, is overcompensating. Playing a vegan, athiest,
pacifist wizard for instance is a ticket to an early death, which means
you don't reall get a chance to learn the tactics before something
kills you.

This is where I get hung up playing wizards: I can't figure out how
much to level up, when. I'm finding that XL 5-6 and DLVL 6-8 are very
dangerous for a wizard. Even if you're playing Pacifist, DLVL 6-8 tends
to be where monsters get dramaticly harder: swarms of orcs,
werecreatures, rothes, etc. Gain a few experience levels and suddenly
you've got yellow lights, ant swarms, and leucrotta.

But I think my concept for playing wizards may be fundamentally flawed.
It's as though I'm missing some key ingredient. I'm prioritizing armor,
collecting daggers, ditching heavy books and inessential potions to
keep unburdened. Letting my pet fight and gain experience. But I'm
still getting hammered. Plus, I invariably have more food problems with
a wizard than with any other class, and I generally don't even cast
spells/wear rings in the early game (the only ring that goes on from
turn one is slow digestion. If I have a protection ring or regeneration
ring, they go on when a fight is unavoidable and come off when I'm
safe/healed).

Any thoughts, Jove?



